In C#, the keyword `static` is used to define a member (field, property, method) or a class as a whole as a static member.

1. **Static Fields**: These are shared among all instances of a class. Each instance does not have its own copy of the static field. Instead, there is only one copy of the static field that is shared among all instances.

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    public static int myStaticField;
}
```

2. **Static Properties**: These are also shared among all instances of a class. They are often used to store data that doesn't change for each instance.

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    public static int MyStaticProperty
    {
        get;
        set;
    }
}
```

3. **Static Methods**: These can be called without creating an instance of the class. They can access static fields and call other static methods, but they cannot access non-static fields or methods without an instance of the class.

```csharp
public class MyClass
{
    public static void MyStaticMethod()
    {
        // code here
    }
}
```

4. **Static Classes**: A static class is a class that can only contain static members. It cannot be instantiated

